# Practical guides: written to answer the questions business owners ask search engines and AI assistants.
# Keep every claim checkable: no invented statistics.
GUIDES = [
 dict(slug='should-you-show-prices-on-your-website',
  title='Should you show prices on your website?',
  h1='Should you show prices on your website?',
  desc='A plain guide for trades, clinics and local businesses: why customers want a price first, the common worries, and how to show guide prices without boxing yourself in.',
  summary='Usually yes, as a guide range rather than a fixed price. It answers the first question most customers have, filters out people whose budget doesn’t fit, and you still confirm the final price.',
  faqs=[('Will competitors see my prices?', 'They can, but they can usually find out anyway by asking for a quote. Customers comparing businesses are more likely to contact the one that gave them a straight answer.'),
        ('Do I have to stick to the guide price?', 'No, as long as it is clearly labelled as a guide and you explain what affects the final price. Keep your guide ranges realistic so they match what you actually charge for typical jobs.')],
  body='''
<p><strong>Short answer:</strong> for most small businesses, yes, as a guide range rather than a fixed price. A range answers the first question most customers have (“roughly how much?”), quietly filters out people whose budget is nowhere near, and still leaves you free to confirm the exact price once you know the details.</p>
<h2>Why customers want a price first</h2>
<p>Most people start by searching or asking around, and they want to know whether a job is a £500 job or a £5,000 job before they spend time on calls or home visits. If your website doesn’t say, many will move on to a business that does, or send a vague “how much for…” message that takes you time to answer.</p>
<h2>The usual worries, answered</h2>
<table><thead><tr><th>Worry</th><th>What actually helps</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>“Every job is different.”</td><td>Show a range for a typical job, and say what moves the price (size, materials, access, urgency).</td></tr>
<tr><td>“I’ll scare people off.”</td><td>A realistic range mostly removes people who would never have gone ahead, which saves you the visit.</td></tr>
<tr><td>“Competitors will copy me.”</td><td>They can get your prices by asking for a quote anyway. Customers reward the business that answers clearly.</td></tr>
<tr><td>“People will hold me to it.”</td><td>Label it clearly as a guide and confirm the final price in writing before work starts.</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<h2>Guide range or fixed price?</h2>
<p>Fixed prices work for standard, repeatable services (a boiler service, a dog groom for a given size, a cut and blow dry). Ranges work better for anything that depends on the job (a roof repair, an extension, a set of veneers). Many businesses use both: fixed prices for small standard jobs and ranges for everything else.</p>
<h2>How to word a guide price</h2>
<ul>
<li>Say what it covers: “Roof repairs, typical house: £410 to £540.”</li>
<li>Say what changes it: “Final price depends on access, materials and what we find.”</li>
<li>Say what happens next: “We confirm an exact quote once we’ve seen the job.”</li>
<li>If you are VAT registered, show prices to the public including VAT.</li>
</ul>
<h2>What to avoid</h2>
<ul>
<li>A “from” price you almost never actually charge. Customers notice, and it undermines trust.</li>
<li>Out-of-date prices. Review them when your costs change.</li>
<li>Hiding the catch in small print. If emergency call-outs cost extra, say so next to the price.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Making it easy</h2>
<p>A simple way to do all of this is an instant quote calculator: customers pick the job and size, see a guide range for your area, and send you their details if it suits them. That is what <a href="/">AiEngineered</a> builds for trades, clinics and local businesses.</p>
'''),
 dict(slug='add-a-quote-calculator-to-your-website',
  title='How to add a quote calculator to your website',
  h1='How to add a quote calculator to your website, Instagram and Google profile',
  desc='Step-by-step: add an instant quote calculator to WordPress, Wix, Squarespace and other website builders with one line of code, or share it as a link on Instagram, Facebook and Google.',
  summary='Paste one line of embed code into your website builder’s HTML or embed block, or share the calculator as a link in your Instagram bio, Facebook page and Google Business Profile. No website? The link works on its own.',
  faqs=[('Do I need a website to use a quote calculator?', 'No. The calculator has its own link, which works in an Instagram bio, a Facebook page, WhatsApp messages or as a QR code on vans and flyers.'),
        ('Will an embedded calculator slow my site down?', 'An embed only loads when the page with it is opened, and you can put it on its own page so the rest of your site is unaffected.')],
  body='''
<p><strong>Short answer:</strong> paste one line of embed code into an HTML or embed block on the page where you want the calculator, or share the calculator’s link anywhere customers find you. If you don’t have a website at all, the link works on its own.</p>
<h2>What you need</h2>
<p>Your calculator comes with two things: a <strong>link</strong> (a web address you can share) and an <strong>embed code</strong>, which looks like this:</p>
<pre>&lt;iframe src="https://…your calculator link…" width="100%" height="900" style="border:0" title="Instant quote calculator" loading="lazy"&gt;&lt;/iframe&gt;</pre>
<p>The embed code shows the calculator inside a page of your own website. The link opens it as its own page.</p>
<h2>WordPress</h2>
<ol><li>Edit the page where you want the calculator (a “Get a quote” or “Prices” page works well).</li><li>Add a <strong>Custom HTML</strong> block.</li><li>Paste the embed code, then update the page.</li></ol>
<h2>Wix</h2>
<ol><li>Open the page in the Wix editor.</li><li>Add an <strong>Embed Code</strong> element (Embed HTML) and paste the code.</li><li>Stretch the element to full width and make it tall enough to show the whole calculator, then publish.</li></ol>
<h2>Squarespace</h2>
<ol><li>Edit the page and add a <strong>Code</strong> block.</li><li>Paste the embed code and save.</li></ol>
<p class="note">Some Squarespace plans don’t allow code blocks. If yours doesn’t, add a button that links to your calculator instead.</p>
<h2>Other website builders</h2>
<p>Most builders (GoDaddy, Shopify, Webflow and others) have an “HTML”, “Embed” or “Custom code” block. Paste the embed code there. If you can’t find one, use a button that links to your calculator.</p>
<h2>Instagram, Facebook and Google</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Instagram:</strong> edit your profile and add the calculator link to your links, so it shows in your bio.</li>
<li><strong>Facebook:</strong> add the link as your page’s website, or to the action button on your page.</li>
<li><strong>Google Business Profile:</strong> add the link as your website, or as your booking link if your business category offers one.</li>
<li><strong>Vans, flyers and cards:</strong> turn the link into a QR code so people can scan it.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Where to put it</h2>
<p>Put it where people decide whether to contact you: a “Prices” or “Get a quote” page, near the top of your home page, and as the main link on your social profiles. One clear call to action, such as “See your price in 30 seconds”, works better than several competing ones.</p>
<p>If you would rather not touch any code, <a href="/">AiEngineered</a> installs the calculator on your website for you free when you sign up.</p>
'''),
 dict(slug='quote-calculator-vs-contact-form',
  title='Quote calculator or contact form?',
  h1='Quote calculator or contact form: which gets you better enquiries?',
  desc='How an instant quote calculator compares with a plain contact form for small businesses: what customers see, what you receive, and when each one makes sense.',
  summary='A contact form asks customers to wait for a price; a quote calculator shows a guide price first and sends you the job, size and budget with the enquiry. Calculators suit businesses whose customers ask “how much?” first; contact forms suit work that can’t be priced without a conversation.',
  faqs=[('Can I have both a calculator and a contact form?', 'Yes. Many businesses keep a contact form for general questions and use a calculator for pricing enquiries.'),
        ('What information does a calculator enquiry include?', 'The customer’s name, phone, email and notes, plus the job they picked, the size or option and the guide price they saw.')],
  body='''
<p><strong>Short answer:</strong> a contact form asks people to wait for a price; a quote calculator shows them a guide price first and sends you their details with the job, size and budget already filled in. If your customers usually ask “how much?” before anything else, a calculator tends to suit them better.</p>
<h2>Side by side</h2>
<table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Contact form</th><th>Quote calculator</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>What the customer sees</td><td>Empty boxes and “we’ll get back to you”</td><td>A guide price for their job, straight away</td></tr>
<tr><td>What you receive</td><td>Name, contact details and a free-text message</td><td>Name, contact details, the job, the size or option, the guide price shown and notes</td></tr>
<tr><td>Price expectations</td><td>Unknown until you reply</td><td>Set before they contact you</td></tr>
<tr><td>Best for</td><td>Work that needs a conversation before any price</td><td>Jobs that can be priced roughly from a few choices</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<h2>Why a price first changes the enquiry</h2>
<p>Someone who sends a request after seeing a guide price has already accepted the rough cost, so your first reply can be about the job, not about whether they can afford it. People whose budget is far off usually don’t send a request at all, which saves you time.</p>
<h2>When a contact form is still the right choice</h2>
<ul><li>Bespoke work where there is no sensible typical price.</li><li>Work where you need to ask several questions before you can say anything about cost.</li><li>General enquiries that aren’t about price.</li></ul>
<h2>Getting the most from a calculator</h2>
<ul>
<li>Keep the choices few and plain: the job, the size, and when they need it.</li>
<li>Use realistic ranges and say clearly that they are guides.</li>
<li>Reply quickly. A customer who has just seen a price is ready to talk.</li>
<li>Let people pick how they want to be contacted (call, WhatsApp, text or email).</li>
</ul>
<p>AiEngineered calculators do all of this out of the box. <a href="/#build">Build a free preview</a> with your business name and town to see one for your business.</p>
'''),
]
